.040679931640625, -0.01800537109375, 0.02471923828125, 0.0135955810546875, 0.01313018798828125, -0.0183258056640625, -0.0220947265625, 0.03131103515625, 0.0242919921875, 0.0224456787109375, -0.036346435546875, -0.000598907470703125, -0.06304931640625, -0.003108978271484375, -0.03515625, 9.834766387939453e-06, -0.0234375, -0.03857421875, 0.001918792724609375, 0.00844573974609375, -0.01373291015625, 0.0022678375244140625, 0.0300140380859375, 0.004383087158203125, -0.007419586181640625, -0.024810791015625, -0.01238250732421875, -0.001071929931640625, -0.0127410888671875, -0.02801513671875, -0.017974853515625, -0.004871368408203125, 0.050323486328125, -0.0024929046630859375, -0.00977325439453125, 0.035430908203125, 0.0293121337890625, 0.04266357421875, 0.0181427001953125, 0.004703521728515625, -0.0311279296875, 0.0283660888671875, 0.01358795166015625, -0.00724029541015625, -0.00684356689453125, 0.0247650146484375, 0.0148773193359375, -0.018524169921875, 0.0038604736328125, 0.00020778179168701172, -0.03350830078125, -0.0099945068359375, -0.00727081298828125, -0.0008940696716308594, 0.006847381591796875, -0.0086669921875, 0.007415771484375, -0.0177459716796875, -0.01471710205078125, 0.0197601318359375, -0.02239990234375, 0.019073486328125, 0.0159759521484375, -0.01204681396484375, 0.04876708984375, -0.03936767578125, 0.007289886474609375, -0.01459503173828125, 0.03656005859375, -0.03424072265625, 0.0298309326171875, 0.0163421630859375, 0.03204345703125, -0.00580596923828125, -0.01177215576171875, 0.038238525390625, 0.00855255126953125, -0.02886962890625, 0.01432037353515625, 0.00714874267578125, -0.0196380615234375, 0.059326171875, 0.01507568359375, 0.0181427001953125, 0.005649566650390625, 0.006381988525390625, 0.006755828857421875, 0.02618408203125, 0.00777435302734375, 0.01198577880859375, -0.01290130615234375, -0.00333404541015625, -0.007785797119140625, 0.034332275390625, 0.0504150390625, 0.051300048828125, -0.023773193359375, -0.0025043487548828125, -0.0012426376342773438, 0.025238037109375, -0.028564453125, -0.01128387451171875, 0.0273284912109375, -0.0034427642822265625, 0.0144805908203125, -0.011383056640625, 0.025482177734375, 0.0447998046875, 0.033782958984375, 0.0230712890625, -0.0116729736328125, 0.0018291473388671875, 0.0227813720703125, 0.03521728515625, -0.0299072265625, -0.003948211669921875, -0.036773681640625, -0.04461669921875, 0.021697998046875, 0.0026874542236328125, 0.0005946159362792969, -0.0079345703125, 0.01004791259765625, 0.0022907257080078125, 0.0092010498046875, -0.0032596588134765625, -0.032012939453125, -0.021087646484375, 0.0021343231201171875, 0.0031642913818359375, 0.024932861328125, -0.00518035888671875, 0.01093292236328125, -0.00441741943359375, 0.0066986083984375, 0.0020503997802734375, -0.00353240966796875, 0.016998291015625, 0.006317138671875, 0.01081085205078125, -0.032379150390625, 0.0019140243530273438, 0.00681304931640625, -0.0176544189453125, -0.004428863525390625, -0.0015554428100585938], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, implementations based on in-house small language models (SLMs)\u2014compact transformer-based models trained for specific tasks\u2014offer potential advantages in cost, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability.\n\n**Objective, Novelty, and Research Questions**\n\nThe main objective of this study is to evaluate the effectiveness of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, with a focus on understanding how the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings relates to classification performance. Our work is novel in two key respects:\n- We extend prior findings on embedding geometry\u2014previously focused on token-level representations in general NLP tasks\u2014to sentence-level embeddings in a financial document classification context.\n- We provide the first empirical analysis linking the geometric properties of sentence embeddings (such as anisotropy and local clustering) to practical classification outcomes for client-specific invoice data. This includes quantifying how vendor identity manifests in embedding space and impacts downstream classification, which has not been systematically explored in prior work.\n\nOur investigation is guided by the following research questions:\n1. Can in-house SLMs, fine-tuned on client-specific data, achieve competitive performance in invoice-to-GL code classification compared to established baselines?\n2. How does the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings (e.g., anisotropy, clustering) relate to classification performance, particularly with respect to vendor identity?\n3. Does augmenting invoice text with structured input (explicit field labels) improve SLM performance?\n4. What are the practical implications of embedding geometry for model deployment and improvement in real-world financial settings?\n\n**Background and Definitions**\n\nWe analyse the pre-trained embedding geometry of a small sentence transformer (SBERT, specifically distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2) and a transformer-based SLM (DeBERTa). For clarity:\n- **Sentence transformer:** A neural network architecture that produces fixed-length vector representations (embeddings) for entire sentences or documents, optimised for semantic similarity tasks.\n- **Classic SLM:** Here, refers to compact transformer-based models (e.g., DeBERTa) that are smaller than large language models (LLMs) and suitable for in-house deployment.\n\n**Embedding Geometry Concepts**\n- **Anisotropy:** The embedding space is unevenly distributed, with certain directions or regions more densely populated. For example, if most invoice embeddings are concentrated along a few axes, the space is anisotropic, which can make it harder for a classifier to distinguish between different categories if they overlap in these dense regions.\n- **Isotropic clusters:** Locally uniform groupings where embeddings are distributed more evenly in all directions\u2014imagine a tight, spherical cluster of points in space, where no direction is preferred.\n\n*Intuitive Example:*  \nConsider a 2D scatter plot of invoice embeddings. If most points are stretched along a diagonal line (anisotropic), distinguishing between categories that overlap along this line is difficult. However, if points from the same vendor form tight, round clusters (isotropic clusters), a classifier can more easily separate them. (See Supplementary Figure 1 for a schematic illustration.)\n\nIn our financial corpus, we find that the sentence-embedding space is globally anisotropic but composed of locally isotropic clusters, and these clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity. This means that while the overall space is uneven, invoices from the same vendor tend to form tight, well-separated groups, which can facilitate accurate classification.\n\n**Justification for Model and Baseline Choices**\n\n- **Model Selection:**  \n  - *SBERT (distiluse-base-multilingual-cased-v2):* Chosen for its efficiency, strong performance on sentence-level tasks, and suitability for deployment in resource-constrained, in-house environments.\n  - *DeBERTa:* Included as a representative of transformer-based SLMs, providing a point of comparison for embedding geometry and classification performance.\n  - These models were chosen over larger LLMs due to considerations of cost, data privacy, and the practical need for models that can be fine-tuned and deployed on-premises.\n\n- **Baselines:**  \n  - *Zero-shot LLM baseline:* OpenAI\u2019s GPT-3.5-turbo, prompted with raw invoice text and a list of possible GL codes, represents a state-of-the-art, general-purpose LLM without fine-tuning.\n  - *Vendor identity classifier:* Assigns the most frequent GL code for each vendor, reflecting a common industry heuristic.\n  - *Traditional ML baseline:* Logistic regression with TF-IDF features, a standard approach in text classification.\n\nWe acknowledge that traditional machine learning classifiers (e.g., logistic regression, random forest) are commonly used in text classification tasks. In our dataset, these models (using TF-IDF or bag-of-words features) underperformed compared to SLM-based approaches, particularly in capturing the nuanced context and structure of invoice text. However, for completeness and transparency, we summarise their results in the main text below.\n\n**Dataset Composition and Limitations**\n\n- **Composition:**  \n  - ~10,000 anonymised invoices from 12 clients (retail, manufacturing, professional services).\n  - Each invoice labelled with the correct GL code (from 45 unique codes) and vendor identity.\n  - Invoice length: median 120 words (range: 30\u2013500).\n  - Mix of recurring and one-off purchases; diversity in invoice types and client business models.\n\n- **Limitations:**  \n  1. **GL code imbalance:** Some categories (e.g., office supplies) are much more frequent than others (e.g., consulting fees).\n  2. **Vendor skew:** A small number of vendors account for a large proportion of invoices.\n  3. **Selection bias:** Data is sourced from clients willing to participate, which may not represent all industry sectors or invoice formats.\n\n*Implications for Generalisability:*  \nThese limitations may affect the external validity of our findings. For example, models may perform less well on rare GL codes, vendors with limited historical data, or invoice formats not represented in our dataset. Caution should be exercised when applying these results to other domains (e.g., healthcare, government) or to less-represented invoice types. We address class imbalance during training via stratified sampling and report per-category performance below.\n\n**Data Preprocessing, Anonymisation, and Potential Confounding**\n\n- **Preprocessing Steps:**  \n  - Removal or pseudonymisation of all personally identifiable information (PII), including names, addresses, account numbers, and invoice numbers.\n  - Vendor names replaced with unique identifiers.\n  - Correction or exclusion of invoices with missing/inconsistent fields.\n  - Standardisation: lowercasing, whitespace removal, normalisation of date and currency formats.\n  - Tokenisation using default SBERT and DeBERTa pipelines.\n\n- **Potential Confounding by Vendor Identity:**  \n  Pseudonymisation of vendor names preserves the signal of vendor identity, which is a strong predictor for GL code assignment. This can lead to confounding, where models rely heavily on vendor identity rather than generalising from invoice content. To assess this, we conducted ablation experiments:\n    - **Ablation Design:** Vendor identifiers were removed from the input text, and models were retrained and evaluated.\n    - **Results:** Performance dropped significantly (see Results section), confirming the importance of vendor information.\n    - **Impact on Generalisability:** Heavy reliance on vendor identity may limit the model\u2019s ability to generalise to new or unseen vendors. In real-world deployment, this is often acceptable since vendor is known at classification time, but for broader applicability, further work is needed.\n    - **Mitigation Strategies:** Possible approaches include:\n      - Evaluating performance on a held-out set of unseen vendors.\n      - Incorporating domain adaptation or meta-learning techniques.\n      - Training models to balance reliance on vendor and content features.\n\n**Methods**\n\n- **Model Training:**  \n  - SBERT fine-tuned on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU (24GB VRAM) for 5 epochs.\n  - Batch size: 32; learning rate: 2e-5.\n  - Hyperparameters selected via grid search: batch sizes [16, 32, 64], learning rates [1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5], epochs [3, 5, 10]. Best configuration chosen based on validation F1 score.\n  - Early stopping based on validation loss (patience=2 epochs).\n  - Train-test split stratified by client to ensure test invoices from each client were not seen during training.\n  - All experiments repeated with three random seeds; results averaged.\n\n- **Ablation Experiments:**  \n  - Vendor identifiers removed from input text to assess reliance on vendor information.\n  - Additional ablation: removal of structured field labels to test impact on performance.\n\n- **Data Augmentation and Regularisation:**  \n  - No synthetic data augmentation was used.\n  - Regularisation via dropout (rate=0.1) and early stopping.\n\n- **Software:**  \n  - Python 3.10, PyTorch 1.13, HuggingFace Transformers 4.26, scikit-learn 1.2.\n\n- **Baselines:**  \n  1. **Zero-shot LLM (GPT-3.5-turbo):** Prompted with invoice text and GL code list; temperature=0 for deterministic outputs.\n  2. **Vendor identity classifier:** Assigns most frequent GL code per vendor.\n  3. **Traditional ML (Logistic Regression with TF-IDF):** Standard scikit-learn implementation; hyperparameters tuned via grid search.\n\n- **Evaluation Metrics:**  \n  - Accuracy and macro-averaged F1 score, reported overall and per GL code category."}}